      "status": "archived",
      "timestamp": 1775655562.8537278,
      "model": "ollama/gemma4:latest",
      "moc_blurb": "This review details the $6.5 billion security breach at Bybit, dissecting a sophisticated cyberattack that compromised the exchange's cold Ethereum wallet. The breach did not stem from a simple hack, but from exploiting critical vulnerabilities within the wallet's multi-signature and upgradeability features.\n\nThe attackers used a malicious contract and a \"delegate call\" to overwrite the wallet's secure logic, enabling the mass draining of assets. While Bybit assures clients of its financial solvency and commitment to covering losses, the incident serves as a stark warning to the entire crypto ecosystem.\n\nThe core finding is the severe risk posed by unchecked \"upgradeability\" in smart contracts. The document urges the industry to urgently enhance security measures, focusing on eliminating or strictly limiting wallet upgradeability features and implementing advanced anomaly detection protocols to prevent future catastrophic losses."
    },
    "MAS TCRS 2026-03 ADVISORY ON STRENGTHENING CYBER SECURITY POSTURE.pdf": {
      "status": "archived",
      "timestamp": 1775700726.021427,
      "model": "ollama/gemma4:latest",
      "moc_blurb": "This MAS advisory mandates a systemic, Board-level overhaul of cyber resilience, shifting financial institutions from a mere compliance mindset to a proactive, risk-focused approach. The core value is safeguarding public confidence and the uninterrupted delivery of financial services against sophisticated cyber threats.\n\nKey mandates require that risk management be embedded across all operations, including rigorous oversight of third-party vendors. Governance must be \"Tone from the Top,\" empowering C-suite leaders to manage cyber risk.\n\nActionable findings include implementing advanced technical controls such as network segmentation, Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA), and deploying Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR). Critically, FIs must validate controls through continuous testing, including penetration testing, Attack Surface Management, and mandatory table-top incident response exercises, ensuring resiliency is proven, not just declared."
    }
